.TH LLAPI_SCAN_NAMESPACE 3 2026-08-18 "Lustre User API" "Lustre Library Functions"
.SH NAME
llapi_scan_namespace \- scan a Lustre namespace, one record per object
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <lustre/lustreapi.h>
.PP
.BI "typedef int (*llapi_scan_cb_t)(const struct llapi_scan_rec *" rec ,
.BI "                               void *" data ");"
.PP
.BI "int llapi_scan_namespace(const char *" path ,
.BI "                         const struct llapi_scan_param *" sp ,
.BI "                         llapi_scan_cb_t " cb ", void *" data ");"
.PP
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B llapi_scan_namespace()
walks the namespace below
.I path
and calls
.I cb
once for each object found, passing it a
.B struct llapi_scan_rec
describing that object and the caller's
.I data
unchanged.
.LP
.I sp
may be NULL for the defaults. Otherwise its
.I lfsp_size
field must be set to
.BR "sizeof(struct llapi_scan_param)" .
The caller must own that many bytes, because the library reads them.
A caller built against an older, shorter definition of the structure is
accepted and the fields it did not set read as zero. A longer one is
accepted too, up to 4096 bytes, as long as every byte past the end of this
library's definition is zero \(em which is the caller saying it set no field
this library could not honour \(em and is refused with \-EINVAL otherwise.
The size is therefore not a version number: an application built against a
newer header runs against an older library for as long as it asks only for
what that library has.
.I lfsp_max_depth
limits the descent, counted as
.BR find (1)
counts it: 1 is the start point and its children, 2 their children in
turn, and 0 is unlimited.
.I lfsp_thread_count
sets the number of scan threads (0 or 1 scans in the calling thread), and
.I lfsp_flags
takes
.B LLAPI_SCAN_F_STOP_ON_ERROR
to abandon the scan when an object cannot be read rather than skipping it.
.I lfsp_padding
must be zero: those bytes are already counted in
.IR lfsp_size ,
so a field carved out of them later can only be refused here, never
noticed.
.LP
.I lfsp_want
is the demand mask: the
.B LLAPI_SCAN_*
fields the consumer will read, or 0 for everything except
.BR LLAPI_SCAN_MDT_INDEX ,
.B LLAPI_SCAN_PROJID
and
.BR LLAPI_SCAN_HSM ,
which each cost an ioctl or an open per object and are gathered only when
named.
.B LLAPI_SCAN_LMV
is not one of them: it costs a second ioctl, but only on a directory,
which is the one place it means anything.
A scan wanting nothing outside
.B LLAPI_SCAN_DIRENT_MASK
performs no ioctl per object, which is what makes a name-only search cheap.
A field not asked for may still be delivered.
.LP
A bit of
.I lfsp_want
this library does not know, or knows but this scan cannot answer for, is
not an error: it is dropped, and
.I lfsp_got
reports what is left. If
.I lfsp_got
is not NULL it is filled, before the first record, with the subset of
.I lfsp_want
this scan will answer for \(em a property of the scan rather than of the
library, since what a scan can answer for depends on the entry point and,
for a scan of a target, on what the target turns out to be. Where
.I lfsp_want
is 0 it reports what the scanner's default came to. A bit in
.I lfsp_got
says the scan may fill the field, not that any one object has it: an object
with no project id still leaves
.B LLAPI_SCAN_PROJID
clear in
.IR lfsr_valid .
So an application can ask an older library for everything it might want and
decide once whether the subset it is offered will do, rather than learning
field by field that a bit never arrives.
.LP
.I lfsp_filter
is an optional pre-filter, called before any I/O on the object with a record
carrying only
.IR lfsr_path ,
.I lfsr_name
and, when the directory entry knew the object type,
.BR LLAPI_SCAN_TYPE .
It returns 0 to gather and deliver the object, 1 to skip it without
gathering (descent into a directory is unaffected), or a negative value to
stop the scan.
.LP
Each field of the record has a bit, one of the
.B LLAPI_SCAN_*
constants, which is set only when the scanner could answer for that field.
A consumer must test the bit rather than compare against zero: a scanner
that cannot answer and a scanner that answers zero mean different things.
.LP
Two masks report those bits, each owning the half of the record it
describes. The fields of
.I lfsr_stx
are reported by
.IR lfsr_stx.stx_mask ,
and the low
.B LLAPI_SCAN_*
constants are the kernel's
.B STATX_*
values, so the two are the same bits under either name. Everything else is
reported by
.IR lfsr_valid ,
whose own low half is reserved and always zero.
.I lfsp_want
takes either half.
.LP
.I lfsr_size
holds the size of the record the scanner filled, so a consumer built against
an older definition of the structure continues to work; fields are only ever
appended.
.LP
.I lfsr_stx
begins the record, so a
.B struct llapi_scan_rec *
may be cast to a
.B struct statx *
and handed to anything that takes one \(em
.BI & rec ->lfsr_stx
says the same thing and costs no more. Every other field therefore sits at an
offset
.BR "sizeof(struct statx)" ,
which the library asserts at build time is still the 256 bytes it has been
since Linux 4.11: a statx that grew would move them under a consumer already
built against the old ones, and
.I lfsr_size
reports how much of a record was filled, never that a field moved.
.LP
For a regular file,
.B LLAPI_SCAN_SIZE
and
.B LLAPI_SCAN_BLOCKS
are set only when the MDT can answer authoritatively. The file data lives on
OSTs, so in the general case the size is not the MDT's to give, and the record
says so rather than reporting zero. When the MDT holds a lazy size-on-MDT
value instead,
.B LLAPI_SCAN_LAZY_SIZE
(and
.BR LLAPI_SCAN_LAZY_BLOCKS )
says so and the value is in
.IR lfsr_stx.stx_size .
.B STATX_SIZE
stays clear for it, as it does in llite when the MDT's answer is not
authoritative.
A consumer that needs a strict size for every regular file must stat it, which
is what
.BR lfs-find (1)
does for
.BR --size ;
.I lfsr_parent_fd
and
.I lfsr_name
are there so it can do so with
.BR fstatat (2).
.I lfsr_parent_fd
is the parent's descriptor only while
.I lfsp_thread_count
is 1: above that each subdirectory is a work unit of its own and the walk
enters it with no parent open, so a directory's record carries \-1. A
consumer that needs the parent above one thread opens
.I lfsr_path
itself.
.LP
The layout is delivered raw, as
.I lfsr_lmm
and
.IR lfsr_lmmsize ,
for the caller to decode with
.BR llapi_layout_get_by_xattr (3).
That call takes a non-const buffer and byte-swaps it in place, so a
consumer copies
.I lfsr_lmmsize
bytes and decodes the copy:
.I lfsr_lmm
points into the scan's own buffer, which the walk reuses for the next
object.
A directory's stripe arrives likewise as
.I lfsr_lmv
and
.IR lfsr_lmvsize ,
under
.BR LLAPI_SCAN_LMV ,
which is set only for a directory that has one.
.LP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_HSM
fills
.I lfsr_hsm_states
from
.B enum hsm_states
and
.IR lfsr_hsm_archive_id ,
for a regular file that has them. State 0 with the bit set means the scan
asked and the file has no HSM state, which is not the same as the bit being
clear.
.SS The record's fields and their bits
Each field is set only when its bit is set, in
.I lfsr_stx.stx_mask
for the fields of
.I lfsr_stx
and in
.I lfsr_valid
for the rest.
.TP 22
.B LLAPI_SCAN_FID
.I lfsr_fid
\(em the object's FID.
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_TYPE
the
.B S_IFMT
part of
.IR lfsr_stx.stx_mode ,
from the directory entry, with no I/O behind it.
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_MODE
.I lfsr_stx.stx_mode
in full.
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_NLINK
.IR lfsr_stx.stx_nlink .
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_UID
.RB / LLAPI_SCAN_GID
.I lfsr_stx.stx_uid
and
.IR lfsr_stx.stx_gid .
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_PROJID
.IR lfsr_projid .
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_SIZE
.RB / LLAPI_SCAN_BLOCKS
.I lfsr_stx.stx_size
and
.IR lfsr_stx.stx_blocks ,
authoritatively; the
.B LAZY_
pair says the value is a size-on-MDT one instead.
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_ATIME
.RB / MTIME / CTIME / BTIME
.IR lfsr_stx.stx_atime ,
.IR lfsr_stx.stx_mtime ,
.I lfsr_stx.stx_ctime
and
.IR lfsr_stx.stx_btime ,
each a whole
.BR "struct statx_timestamp" .
The MDT reports whole seconds today, so
.I tv_nsec
is 0; the field is there for when it reports more.
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_ATTRS
.IR lfsr_stx.stx_attributes ,
set when the object's flags were answered \(em
.B OBD_MD_FLFLAGS
in the MDT's reply, which it sets only when it filled them. The value is
masked down to the
.B STATX_ATTR_*
bits named in
.IR lfsr_stx.stx_attributes_mask ,
which is the scanner's own declaration of what it can represent; flags
outside it are masked off rather than reported. This bit is in
.I lfsr_valid
because statx has no mask bit of its own for
.IR stx_attributes .
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_LAYOUT
.I lfsr_lmm
and
.IR lfsr_lmmsize .
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_LMV
.I lfsr_lmv
and
.IR lfsr_lmvsize ,
with
.B LLAPI_SCAN_LMV_FOREIGN
set beside it when the buffer is a
.B struct lmv_foreign_md
(declared in
.BR <linux/lustre/lustre_idl.h> )
rather than an
.BR lmv_user_md .
Naming
.B LLAPI_SCAN_LMV_FOREIGN
in
.I lfsp_want
asks for
.B LLAPI_SCAN_LMV
too, and
.I lfsp_got
reports both.
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_MDT_INDEX
.IR lfsr_mdt_index .
.TP
.B LLAPI_SCAN_HSM
.I lfsr_hsm_states
and
.IR lfsr_hsm_archive_id .
.LP
.I lfsr_path
and
.I lfsr_name
are always set.
.I lfsr_parent_fd
is an open descriptor on the parent directory and
.I lfsr_fd
one on the object, or \-1 when the scan holds none; neither is the
consumer's to close, and both are valid only for the duration of the
callback.
.SS A target that is not Lustre
.I path
does not have to name a Lustre filesystem, and a walk that crosses onto one
that is not \(em a bind mount, a local disk below the mount point \(em does
not stop there. Attributes then come from
.BR stat (2)
instead of the MDT, and the record says so: a field that source cannot answer
leaves its bit clear rather than reading as zero. In particular a
.B stat
has no FID, so
.B LLAPI_SCAN_FID
is clear and
.I lfsr_fid
must not be read; the same holds for
.BR LLAPI_SCAN_LAYOUT ,
.BR LLAPI_SCAN_LMV ,
.B LLAPI_SCAN_MDT_INDEX
and
.BR LLAPI_SCAN_HSM .
.LP
What such an object does carry is what a
.B stat
answers \(em type, mode, link count, owner, size, blocks and the three times
\(em plus
.B LLAPI_SCAN_PROJID
where
.I lfsp_want
names it and the filesystem supports project quotas. It is the same walk with
.BR stat (2)
as the attribute source, not a second scanner.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.B llapi_scan_namespace()
returns:
.TP
0
on success.
.TP
<0
a negative errno on failure, or the negative value
.I cb
or
.I lfsp_filter
returned to stop the scan, passed back unchanged. The two are not
distinguishable here: a filter stops only by returning a negative, its
positive values meaning skip. A consumer that must tell them apart should
stop from
.I cb
instead, whose positive values come back under
.B >0
below, or pick a value this page's ERRORS does not list.
.TP
>0
the value a callback returned to stop the scan, unchanged.
.SH ERRORS
.TP 15
.B -EINVAL
.I path
or
.I cb
is NULL, or
.I lfsp_size
is smaller than the fields up to and including
.IR lfsp_want ,
or is larger than 4096 bytes, or is larger than the library's definition of
the parameter structure and a byte past the end of it is not zero, or
.I lfsp_flags
sets a bit this library does not define, or
.I lfsp_padding
is not zero. Neither changes
.IR lfsp_size ,
so refusing them is what gives flags and the reserved bytes the same
guarantee fields already have: a caller never has one silently ignored.
.TP 15
.B -ENAMETOOLONG
.I path
is longer than PATH_MAX.
.SH NOTES
With
.I lfsp_thread_count
greater than 1 the callback is invoked concurrently from several threads and
must be thread-safe. The record passed to each call is private to that call;
.I data
is shared between them.
.SS Lifetimes
The record is valid only for the duration of the callback, and so is
everything it points at. A consumer that keeps any part of either must copy
it. What follows says when each part stops being the object's, because the
answers differ and two of them are shorter than the rule above suggests.
.LP
The record itself is a local of the scan's own traversal callback, so the
fields held by value \(em
.IR lfsr_stx ,
.IR lfsr_fid ,
.I lfsr_projid
and the rest \(em are gone when the callback returns. Copying the structure
is enough for those, and enough for nothing else.
.LP
.I lfsr_path
is the traversal's path buffer, rewritten as the walk moves on. For a
directory that is its own descent: the buffer has the first child appended to
it before any callback below it runs, so a directory's path does not last
even until its next sibling.
.I lfsr_name
points into that buffer rather than beside it, and does not outlive it.
.LP
.I lfsr_lmm
and
.I lfsr_lmv
are the buffers the MDT's answer arrives in, reused from one object to the
next rather than one per object. Within the callback the record is honest about
them: an object with no layout leaves
.I lfsr_lmm
NULL rather than pointing at the last object's bytes. The buffer itself is
rewritten by the
.B next
object's gather, though, which is later than the callback returns \(em so a
pointer kept past that describes whichever object was gathered most recently,
and the gather clears the layout's magic without clearing the body behind it.
With
.I lfsp_thread_count
above 1 a kept pointer can be freed memory instead:
each subdirectory is walked with buffers of its own,
released when that subdirectory is done.
Treat either as an accident of the implementation and not as room.
.LP
.I lfsr_fd
is the shortest-lived. For a directory it is the traversal's own descriptor
and lasts while the subtree is walked. For anything else it is a descriptor
the gather opened only because
.I lfsp_want
asked for a field an ioctl answers, and it is closed as soon as the callback
returns \(em so a kept copy names a closed descriptor whose number the scan
is about to reuse.
.I lfsr_parent_fd
is the parent directory's and lasts while that directory is walked. Neither
is the consumer's to close.
.SH EXAMPLES
To count the regular files under a directory, testing the validity mask
before reading a field:
.EX
        static int count_cb(const struct llapi_scan_rec *rec, void *data)
        {
                __u64 *n = data;
\&
                if ((rec->lfsr_stx.stx_mask & STATX_TYPE) &&
                    S_ISREG(rec->lfsr_stx.stx_mode))
                        (*n)++;
                return 0;
        }
\&
        __u64 n = 0;
        int rc = llapi_scan_namespace("/mnt/lustre", NULL, count_cb, &n);
.EE
.SH AVAILABILITY
.B llapi_scan_namespace()
is part of the
.BR lustre (7)
user application interface library since release 2.18.0.
.\" Added in commit v2_17_57-xxx-gxxxxxxxxx
.SH SEE ALSO
